WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:06.852
Part one stopped at the staircase U.

2
00:00:06.852 --> 00:00:09.158
Go one step further

3
00:00:09.158 --> 00:00:13.659
row one minus row two is one, zero, two

4
00:00:13.659 --> 00:00:14.866
minus one.

5
00:00:14.866 --> 00:00:17.940
This is the reduced form R.

6
00:00:18.290 --> 00:00:21.078
In R, each pivot column is a column

7
00:00:21.158 --> 00:00:24.583
of the identity: one, zero, zero, and zero

8
00:00:24.583 --> 00:00:25.459
one, zero.

9
00:00:25.459 --> 00:00:28.327
Elimination did change the columns

10
00:00:28.327 --> 00:00:31.194
A's first column was one, two, one.

11
00:00:31.194 --> 00:00:34.142
But it did not change the nullspace

12
00:00:34.142 --> 00:00:36.850
because every step can be undone.

13
00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:41.286
Now the equations read off directly.

14
00:00:41.286 --> 00:00:45.999
x one equals minus two x three plus x four.

15
00:00:45.999 --> 00:00:50.390
x two equals minus x three minus x four.

16
00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:53.305
So the special solutions sit

17
00:00:53.397 --> 00:00:55.687
in the free columns of R

18
00:00:55.687 --> 00:00:57.886
with the signs flipped.

19
00:00:57.886 --> 00:01:00.726
Free column three is two, one

20
00:01:00.726 --> 00:01:03.932
s one has pivot entries minus two

21
00:01:03.932 --> 00:01:04.940
minus one.

22
00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:08.238
Free column four is minus one, one

23
00:01:08.238 --> 00:01:10.620
s two has one, minus one.

24
00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:13.402
Stack them as the columns of

25
00:01:13.488 --> 00:01:16.875
the nullspace matrix N: minus F on top

26
00:01:16.875 --> 00:01:18.612
the identity below.

27
00:01:18.612 --> 00:01:21.825
A times N is zero, column by column

28
00:01:21.825 --> 00:01:24.170
as we checked in part one.

29
00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:27.603
A two by two example we can draw.

30
00:01:27.603 --> 00:01:31.147
The matrix with rows one, two and three

31
00:01:31.147 --> 00:01:31.579
six.

32
00:01:31.579 --> 00:01:33.740
Each row is an equation

33
00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:37.370
so each row is a line through the origin

34
00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:39.964
x one plus two x two is zero

35
00:01:39.964 --> 00:01:43.421
and three x one plus six x two is zero

36
00:01:43.421 --> 00:01:45.150
the very same line.

37
00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:50.017
Eliminate: row two minus three times row

38
00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:53.292
one leaves zero equals zero

39
00:01:53.292 --> 00:01:57.244
an equation every point satisfies.

40
00:01:57.244 --> 00:02:02.100
One pivot, so r is one, and x two is free.

41
00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:06.616
Set x two to one: then x one is minus two.

42
00:02:06.616 --> 00:02:10.584
The special solution is minus two, one

43
00:02:10.584 --> 00:02:13.857
and its multiples fill the line

44
00:02:13.857 --> 00:02:16.634
n minus r is two minus one

45
00:02:16.634 --> 00:02:19.610
one special solution, a line.

46
00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:22.421
Change the six to a five.

47
00:02:22.421 --> 00:02:24.296
Now the second line

48
00:02:24.296 --> 00:02:27.955
three x one plus five x two equals zero

49
00:02:27.955 --> 00:02:29.026
tilts away.

50
00:02:29.026 --> 00:02:32.150
The faint line is the old picture.

51
00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:33.445
Eliminate

52
00:02:33.445 --> 00:02:36.845
the second pivot is five minus six

53
00:02:36.845 --> 00:02:37.884
minus one.

54
00:02:37.884 --> 00:02:42.040
Two pivots, r is two, so n minus r is zero

55
00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:43.740
no free variable.

56
00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:47.424
The two lines meet only at the origin

57
00:02:47.424 --> 00:02:51.580
and the nullspace is the zero vector alone.

58
00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:55.040
A free column does not have

59
00:02:55.147 --> 00:02:57.173
to be the last one.

60
00:02:57.173 --> 00:03:01.653
Take B with rows one, three, one and two

61
00:03:01.653 --> 00:03:02.933
six, three.

62
00:03:02.933 --> 00:03:07.307
Row two minus two times row one is zero

63
00:03:07.307 --> 00:03:08.480
zero, one.

64
00:03:08.830 --> 00:03:13.369
So the pivots are in columns one and three.

65
00:03:13.369 --> 00:03:16.325
The free variable is x two

66
00:03:16.325 --> 00:03:18.753
the one in the middle.

67
00:03:18.753 --> 00:03:23.186
Its special solution is minus three, one

68
00:03:23.186 --> 00:03:23.820
zero.

69
00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:25.403
Check row one

70
00:03:25.403 --> 00:03:28.992
minus three plus three plus zero.

71
00:03:28.992 --> 00:03:33.109
Row two: minus six plus six plus zero.

72
00:03:33.109 --> 00:03:34.270
Both zero.

73
00:03:34.850 --> 00:03:35.731
Now count.

74
00:03:35.819 --> 00:03:38.462
Every column is either a pivot

75
00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:40.664
column or a free column

76
00:03:40.752 --> 00:03:43.483
so the number of free variables

77
00:03:43.571 --> 00:03:44.717
is n minus r.

78
00:03:44.717 --> 00:03:48.505
A matrix with m rows has at most m pivots

79
00:03:48.505 --> 00:03:49.650
one per row.

80
00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:52.888
So a wide matrix, three by five

81
00:03:52.979 --> 00:03:55.506
has at most three pivots and

82
00:03:55.596 --> 00:03:57.943
at least two free columns

83
00:03:57.943 --> 00:04:00.560
a nonzero nullspace, always.

84
00:04:00.910 --> 00:04:04.493
What about a tall matrix, five by three?

85
00:04:04.493 --> 00:04:07.538
Must it have a nonzero nullspace?

86
00:04:07.538 --> 00:04:09.150
Pause and decide.

87
00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:09.958
No.

88
00:04:09.958 --> 00:04:13.143
It can have a pivot in every column

89
00:04:13.143 --> 00:04:15.381
and then nothing is free

90
00:04:15.381 --> 00:04:17.791
the nullspace is just zero.

91
00:04:17.791 --> 00:04:20.975
Tall matrices may have free columns

92
00:04:20.975 --> 00:04:22.180
but need not.

93
00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:27.954
Where this shows up

94
00:04:28.064 --> 00:04:32.672
a linear model with weights theta predicts

95
00:04:32.782 --> 00:04:35.964
X theta on its training data.

96
00:04:35.964 --> 00:04:40.681
Here X has two examples and three weights

97
00:04:40.681 --> 00:04:45.289
so it is wide, and its nullspace contains

98
00:04:45.399 --> 00:04:48.800
n equal to minus two, one, two.

99
00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:52.026
With theta equal to one, one, one

100
00:04:52.026 --> 00:04:55.749
the predictions are one plus two plus zero

101
00:04:55.749 --> 00:04:59.133
three, and one plus zero plus one, two.

102
00:04:59.133 --> 00:05:02.432
Add n: theta plus n is minus one, two

103
00:05:02.432 --> 00:05:03.024
three.

104
00:05:03.024 --> 00:05:06.493
The predictions are minus one plus four

105
00:05:06.493 --> 00:05:09.707
three, and minus one plus three, two.

106
00:05:09.707 --> 00:05:11.230
Exactly the same.

107
00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:14.322
The training data cannot tell the

108
00:05:14.405 --> 00:05:16.482
two weight vectors apart.

109
00:05:16.482 --> 00:05:19.555
But a new input, one, one, one, can

110
00:05:19.639 --> 00:05:21.799
it gives three for one and

111
00:05:21.882 --> 00:05:23.460
four for the other.

112
00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:27.319
An assistant gives two special solutions

113
00:05:27.401 --> 00:05:29.122
for the drone matrix.

114
00:05:29.122 --> 00:05:32.482
Multiply the first, two, one, one, zero

115
00:05:32.482 --> 00:05:35.597
by A: two plus one plus three is six

116
00:05:35.597 --> 00:05:38.876
four plus three plus seven is fourteen

117
00:05:38.876 --> 00:05:41.580
two plus two plus four is eight.

118
00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:42.400
Not zero.

119
00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:47.637
The second, minus one, one, zero, one

120
00:05:47.637 --> 00:05:50.981
gives zero, two, and two.

121
00:05:50.981 --> 00:05:52.910
Also not zero.

122
00:05:53.260 --> 00:05:56.631
The count was right: four minus two is two.

123
00:05:56.710 --> 00:05:59.689
But the pivot entries were copied from

124
00:05:59.767 --> 00:06:02.041
R without flipping the signs.

125
00:06:02.041 --> 00:06:05.020
Multiply every claimed solution by A.

126
00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:09.502
So: in R, the special solutions are the free

127
00:06:09.591 --> 00:06:12.340
columns with the signs flipped.

128
00:06:12.340 --> 00:06:14.912
There are n minus r of them

129
00:06:14.912 --> 00:06:16.952
one per free variable

130
00:06:17.041 --> 00:06:20.145
and for the two by two example that

131
00:06:20.233 --> 00:06:23.160
was one special solution, a line.

132
00:06:23.510 --> 00:06:27.173
A wide matrix always has a free variable.

133
00:06:27.173 --> 00:06:28.960
A tall one may not.

134
00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:30.657
Your turn: find R

135
00:06:30.747 --> 00:06:33.784
the rank and the special solutions

136
00:06:33.873 --> 00:06:37.358
of the matrix with rows one, minus one

137
00:06:37.358 --> 00:06:40.485
two and three, minus three, seven.

138
00:06:40.485 --> 00:06:43.790
Next lecture adds a right hand side.
